    explanation: "Knowing exactly how many pallets an order requires is essential for booking transport, quoting freight, and planning warehouse space. This pallet count calculator multiplies the units per layer by the number of layers to find how many units fit on one pallet, then divides your total unit count by that figure and rounds up to give the number of pallets you need.\n\nThe rounding matters: because you cannot ship a fraction of a pallet, any remainder pushes the total up to the next whole pallet. A shipment of 500 cases that fills pallets of 50 needs exactly 10 pallets, but 501 cases would need 11, with the last pallet nearly empty. Seeing this clearly helps you adjust order quantities to fill pallets efficiently and avoid paying to move mostly-empty ones.\n\nThe calculation works because a pallet's capacity is simply its footprint times its stacked height in cases, and the ceiling function reflects the physical reality that partial pallets still occupy a full pallet position in a truck. Warehouse managers, shippers, and buyers use pallet counts to reserve the right number of truck spaces, estimate loading time, and confirm a full truckload or LTL shipment will physically fit before the goods are dispatched.",
