    formula: "density = weight ÷ (length × width × height ÷ 1,728)",
    formulaExplanation: "Freight density is weight divided by volume in cubic feet. Multiplying the three dimensions in inches gives cubic inches, and dividing by 1,728 converts to cubic feet because a cubic foot contains twelve inches cubed. Dividing weight by that volume yields pounds per cubic foot. The formula works because density describes how much mass fills a given space, and less-than-truckload carriers use it to assign the freight class that sets your rate.",
    explanation: "Freight density measures how much your shipment weighs relative to the space it occupies, and it is the single most important number in the less-than-truckload world because it largely determines your freight class and therefore your rate. This freight density calculator multiplies your shipment's length, width, and height in inches, converts that to cubic feet, and divides the weight by the volume to give density in pounds per cubic foot.\n\nDensity matters because LTL carriers classify freight from class 50 for dense, easy-to-handle goods up to class 500 for light, bulky items. A high density means more weight in less space, which typically earns a lower, cheaper class, while a low density signals a bulky load that costs more to move. Getting the figure right helps you quote accurately and avoid costly reclassification charges after pickup.\n\nThe calculation works because dividing weight by volume gives the density that classification tables are built around, and dividing cubic inches by 1,728 simply converts to the cubic feet those tables use. Shippers, freight brokers, and warehouse teams calculate density before booking so they can predict the freight class, compare carrier quotes fairly, and package goods to hit a more favourable class where possible.",
